01-Simulation-Design-Blueprint
Title, ProjectSim Simulation Design Blueprint, version1. Section1 is the executive summary. Vision is an AI-powered project management simulation where learners gain hands-on PM experience by running realistic projects end-to-end. Guiding principles are business before project, story before tasks, consequences matter, no resets, and AI supports but doesn't puppeteer. Learning philosophy is experience first, reflect, then improve, not memorize and test. PMBOK is embedded through project work. Simulation philosophy is three aligned layers. Story, project lifecycle, learning. The core architecture is business case to simulation orchestrator, then project state then event, memory, and relationship engines, then AI stakeholders, then scoring, and Maya coach. Next section after this will define the business case framework with company structure, objectives, and success criteria. That foundation will drive all future business cases.

Part two defines the business case framework. This is where every simulation begins. Every case starts with a real company. Name, industry, size, strategy, and current problems. Followed by a clear business problem statement and opportunity. Then define measurable objectives and success criteria. Then outline the organizational structure and stakeholder ecosystem, who holds power, and who influences whom. Then list constraints and initial risks, and close the section with the business value dashboard, baseline KPIs the learner will be trying to move. That gives every future decision context. Before the project even starts, the learner understands why the project exists and what business success looks like.
Then the next section is the project design. It explains how a real project roadmap gets translated into a seven chapter story. So, the real project roadmap is business case, charter, kickoff, requirements, planning, execution, testing, deployment, close. That's mapped into seven simulation days, each a meaningful period not a calendar day. Each day starts with a briefing, has proactive stakeholder activity, requires real PM tasks, produces consequences, and ends with reflection and a cliffhanger. The section also defines that the simulation engine maintains a continuous project state so that emails, meetings, stakeholder trust, and KPIs evolve day to day. So it never feels like a set of disconnected lessons.
The next section is the simulation world design. This is where the business case becomes a living organization. Every stakeholder gets a real profile, personality, and objectives that might conflict with each other. Relationships have memory, so if you miss something on day 2, it might come back on day 5. Communication preferences matter. Executives want short summaries, while engineering wants detail. And stakeholders don't just wait, they proactively reach out, ask for updates, and escalate. And then the world keeps moving with business events like market shifts or budget cuts so that the project feels like part of a larger company ecosystem.
Then the next section defines the simulation engine, which is basically the brain of ProjectSim. It takes in what the user does, updates the project state, triggers downstream effects, and feeds that into future events and stakeholder behaviors. It's what prevents this from feeling linear. So two learners could take the same business case and end up with very different outcomes. It includes decision engines, dependency logic, consequence modeling, and recovery paths. And then, it adds adaptive difficulty so that as the learner improves, the simulation introduces more complexity. More stakeholders, conflicting priorities, tighter constraints.
the next piece is the learning and assessment framework. Every activity is mapped to competencies and PMBOK domains. But the user never feels like they are taking a test. The assessment is continuous, based on decisions, tradeoffs, communication quality, and outcomes. Maya provides coaching that feels like mentorship, not grading. At the end of each day, there's a reflection. And at the end of the simulation, there's an executive performance review that summarizes what you achieved, where you struggled, and why it mattered.
communication and collaboration framework. Since project management is mostly communication, this becomes the main gameplay surface. Email, chat, meetings, executive updates, one-on-ones, difficult conversations, negotiation moments, everything. Each exchange is scored quietly for clarity, tone, timing, and audience fit. Also, collaboration is not just you talking to AI, it's stakeholders talking to each other, and pulling you into their dynamics. That creates that I'm in the room feeling. Over time, you get communication analytics, not for vanity, but to show patterns like slow response times or over-communicating detail to executives. It's teaching that communication is strategy, not just admin.
A content and scenario library so that every business case is - is reusable, a technical architecture section translating all this into services and data models, an analytics and reporting section for learner and instructor dashboards, and a platform roadmap that shows how you go from single player MVP to multiplayer, instructor modes, and a marketplace. Then the appendices hold the PMBOK mapping, competency definitions, event catalogs, and so on. And together those documents make it possible to build new business cases without breaking the experience every time. So the full documentation set is Blueprint, Content Bible, Simulation Engine spec, Data and API spec, and a content authoring guide. That combination gives your team a shared source of truth and makes scaling realistic.
some governance and authoring layer. Basically, rules for how new business cases get created, so that every new simulation feels consistent, even if it's a different industry. That means templates for business cases, standards for balancing story, project, and learning, quality guidelines, and versioning. Long term, it also sets you up to use AI to help generate first drafts of new scenarios, with human review on top. Then the blueprint closes with a summary of the four layers: business, simulation, learning, and technology, and how they support each other. After that, the next practical step is building out the content Bible, day-by-day story, and all artifacts, because that's what turns this design into something you can actually play.
